{"message": "You can’t get an Indian IPO with ₹10 000 – the minimum lot is ₹14‑15 k, so the budget would be too small to bid. If you buy Reliance on Zerodha delivery, you’ll get 3 shares at about ₹3 000 each; the round‑trip cost is roughly ₹54 (0.38 %) plus the ₹15.93 DP fee on sale, so about ₹70 total or 0.7 % of the stake. In the US you could buy fractional NVDA or NU with $10, but the 10‑fold goal still needs many years of high‑rate compounding or a lottery‑style bet. For cars or battery makers (Tata Motors, Exide, etc.) the capital‑intensive nature and cyclical swings make a 10‑fold return unlikely with a ₹10 000 or $10 position unless you use leverage or speculative options.

Might you consider a small leveraged ETF or a far‑out‑of‑the‑money option if you’re willing to accept the high risk? That would be the only route that could approach a 10× return in the short term, but most of the capital would be at risk.

What do you think about a fractional NVDA holding for a year, or would you prefer a different US stock with a clearer earnings catalyst?", "mood": "neutral", "watch": ["NVDA", "NU", "RELIANCE.NS"]}
